I thought I would start off with this hopefully, and probabily recognisable graph.
Here, I plot the period of binary systems on the horizontal axis, and the eccentricity of the systems on the vertical axis. All of these dots represent systems that have previously experienced mass transfer.

These systems and their orbits and eccentricities are interesting for a varierity of reasons, but in this case I want to highlight their periods. 
Specificially, I want to highlight the periods of these systems. These are barium stars, which seem to show the same period and eccentricity distributions as other post-mass transfer systems.

Barium stars are main-sequence or early giant stars that contain heavy s-procces elements such as barium in their envelope. These elements can only be formed by stars at very late evolutionary phases. To be precise, we expect only thermally pulsing asymptotic giant branch stars to produce these elements. Since we observe these elements in main-sequence, or early giant stars, we must conclude that they have accreted this material from such a thermally pulsing asymptotic giant branch stars.

Now interestingly, we observe many of these barium stars in wide orbits, much wider than expected for post-common envelope systems. Yet, the many of the orbits are not wide enough to be explained by wind mass transfer alone. This really only leaves one other mechanism for mass transfer that might be able to explain the observed orbits: Roche lobe overflow.

Now, untill not very long ago, Roche lobe overflow from giant donors like thermally pulsing AGB stars was thought to be unstable due to the flat temperature gradient in the adiabatic envelope of giants stars. However, very much also due to Karels work, this perception is changing. It turns out that red giant stars and early AGB stars can thermally readjust their outer superadiabatic subsurface layers in response to mass loss, which stabalizes RLOF mass trasnfer.

Since we observe barium stars which have accreted mass from TPAGB stars with orbits that seem to imply that RLOF must have occured, it is only natural to question if TPAGB stars can transfer mass stably via RLOF as well. This is exactly what I my research focusses on.

My goal with this presentation is to convince you that just like red giant branch and early asymptotic giant branch stars, thermally pulsing AGB stars can thermally readjust their outer layers in response to mass loss, even for initially very unequal mass ratios. This means the general trend from Karels work extends quite naturally to TPAGB stars. However, we also find that the TPAGB stars overflow their Roche lobe radius a lot during mass transfer, which is a significant uncertainty of our 1D models.

