### ### ### ###
METHODS
### ### ### ###

Okay, let's take a look at my methods. How do I actually get to my results.
To start, let me say that my work primarily uses the 1D stellar evolution code MESA, which is a great tool. Not only can it simulate nearly all complicated stellar evolution phases imaginable, it can also simulate binary systems. As long as you use the correct options and parameters, MESA is really versitile. The tricky part however, is mostly in finding these correct settings. Especially for complicated phases like the thermally pulsing asymptotic giant branch phase, MESA can easily experience convergence problems, or instabilities. 

Luckily for me however, a lot of people have already done a bunch of work and compiled usefull options and custom subroutines to make MESA behave stably for TP-AGB stars. In particular, I am using the options and routines provided by Rees in 2024 for my TP-AGB stars. I am however using a slightly different wind mass loss prescription, namely the prescription by vassiliadis and wood and I am using a constant wind velocity of 15 km/s.

Now, my workflow works roughly like this:
First, I simulate a single star using the options described by Rees. This TPAGB star will experience many pulses untill it eventually expels its entire envelope after which it will become a white dwarf. The pulses can be seen in this figure.
For every pulse, I save a model that I can later use as a starting point for a binary evolution simulation.

I then use a post-processing step, where I use the single star, together with an initial mass ratio, orbital separation and spin to determine how tides and mass loss will affect the orbit. In particular, I pay a lot of attention to the evolution of the Roche lobe radius of the TPAGB star. 

Using this Roche lobe radius, an efficient binary starting model can be selected. I then use the binary separtion, stellar spin and mass-ratio of the system at the point of the starting model as starting conditions for the detailed MESA models. This ensures the MESA models start with their expected values based on ZAMS initial values.

Importantly, the MESA models and the post processing step use the same tidal and wind mass transfer prescription. In both cases I use a standard hut-style tidal prescription, where I use a slightly different k over T ratio as described by Preece in 2022. I then use a wind mass transfer prescription from Saladino which is based on hydrodynamical simulations of wind mass transfer specifically from TPAGB stars.

Lastly, I use the binary evolution options from Karel to make sure mass transfer is properly resolved.

Okay! Now that we know how simulate my models, Let's take a look at some results. Since I have been showing of this example system that start with a Roche lobe radius of approximately 550 solar radii, let's see how this one evolves through RLOF!


